# Every text file is stored LF and checked out LF, on every platform.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING FOR THE GUARD TESTS, not a formatting preference.
#
# Git-for-Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which checks out this
# repository's 1,247 LF-stored text files as CRLF. The static-analysis guards in
# tests/unit — the ones enforcing hard rule 3, service-role tenancy scoping and
# "every server action calls an auth gate" — work by readFileSync-ing source and
# asserting on its text. Several assert on multi-line strings containing \n:
#
#   expect(ENGINE).toContain("await finish(db, runId, 'failed')\n    return {")
#
# Against a CRLF working tree that match fails, the guard goes red for a reason
# that has nothing to do with the code, and a developer learns to filter it out.
# A guard that is routinely ignored is a guard that fails OPEN — the vacuous
# guard defect class already recorded twice in docs/PROGRESS.md.
#
# The index already holds LF for all of these, so normalising the working tree
# changes no committed content.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never examined as text, never line-ending converted.
*.png  -text
*.jpg  -text
*.webp -text

# ⚠️ Two files are binary despite text-shaped extensions. Both are deliberate
# and both must survive byte-identical.
#
# binary-renamed.html is an MS-DOS executable — one of the hostile fixtures
# CLAUDE.md requires ("binary renamed .html"). Normalising it would defeat the
# content-sniffing test that consumes it.
tests/fixtures/html/binary-renamed.html -text
tests/unit/parse.test.ts                -text
